From 9deeb8c84eaa2351083afe9cbf165f96c3b8d434 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:56:23 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] perf(desktop): share identical in-flight backend
 reads instead of resending them

The Desktop on laptop-1 logged 83 "Timed out connecting to Hermes backend" in 30 min (card
t_01a1d5fd92ac3a4d), 72 on conn=hermes-hub, 40 of those in 8 shared 2-second bursts across 21 paths.

Five whys
1. Why did requests time out? They queued past their 8-15 s deadline on the SSH tunnel to the hub, in
   bursts that hit every path at once (the paths that time out are victims, not the cause).
2. Why did the tunnel queue? A 30 s loopback pcap of the hub's backend port (tcpdump -i lo port 9147,
   2026-11-13 17:45 UTC) shows 101 requests returning 9.5 MB to this one Desktop, ~320 KB/s, on a link
   with 20% ICMP loss: each burst is MBs of session lists queued ahead of small reads.
3. Why so many bytes? 44% of them (4.1 MB) were repeats of a GET still in flight: the same
   /api/profiles/sessions?...profile=all list up to 6 times within 1.5 s (121-273 KB each), the
   unfiltered /api/sessions list 3x, the 508 KB project tree 2x.
4. Why repeats? Each caller (sidebar slices, cron list, per-profile merges, each window) goes through
   fetchJsonForBackend on its own; the delta-read layer only shrinks a repeat against a base it already
   holds, and no layer shares a read that has not answered yet.
5. Why wasn't it caught? Nothing measured wire duplicates; the timeout detector counts victim paths,
   and earlier fixes thinned single pollers rather than the shared sink.

Fix: fetchJsonForBackend (the single sink for renderer-routed and main-process backend REST calls) now
goes through read-coalescing.ts. A bodyless GET identical to one in flight (same backend origin,
credential, headers, gateway mode, path, deadline, pool, profile scope) waits for that one's answer;
each caller gets its own structuredClone copy, so one caller's edits (tagRemoteSessionRows) cannot leak
into another's. Any write to a backend bumps its generation, so a read issued after a write never joins
a read sent before it. Errors are shared once and the next read asks again.

Tests: electron/read-coalescing.test.ts (behaviour: one wire request for 10 identical concurrent reads
with distinct copies; settled reads ask again; every key part separates; writes never coalesce and
read-after-write sees the write; shared failure then retry; GET-only), plus a main.ts wiring check that
the uncoalesced transport has no other caller. Mutant (coalescing disabled) fails 2 of 6. Wiring tests in
local-gateway/remote-down-gate/slow-link now slice the renamed transport body fetchJsonForBackendOnce.
